        <!-- Section: Simplification Table -->
        <section class="article-section" id="section-simplification">
            <div class="article-container">
                <div class="section-eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow-icon"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="m12 3-1.9 5.8a2 2 0 0 1-1.3 1.3L3 12l5.8 1.9a2 2 0 0 1 1.3 1.3L12 21l1.9-5.8a2 2 0 0 1 1.3-1.3L21 12l-5.8-1.9a2 2 0 0 1-1.3-1.3z"/></svg></span> Simplification</div>
                <h2 class="article-h2">Square Root Simplification Table</h2>
                <p>Not every square root is a perfect square, but many can still be simplified into a smaller, cleaner form by pulling out any perfect-square factors.</p>
                <div class="table-scroll-wrapper">
                    <div class="table-scroll-hint">
                        <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M18 8L22 12L18 16"/><path d="M6 8L2 12L6 16"/><line x1="2" y1="12" x2="22" y2="12"/></svg>
                        Scroll horizontally →
                    </div>
                    <table class="data-table data-table--compact">
                        <thead><tr><th>Expression</th><th>Simplified Form</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td>√8</td><td>2√2</td><td>8 = 4 × 2, and √4 = 2</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>√12</td><td>2√3</td><td>12 = 4 × 3</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>√18</td><td>3√2</td><td>18 = 9 × 2</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>√20</td><td>2√5</td><td>20 = 4 × 5</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>√50</td><td>5√2</td><td>50 = 25 × 2</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>√72</td><td>6√2</td><td>72 = 36 × 2</td></tr>
                            <tr><td>√98</td><td>7√2</td><td>98 = 49 × 2</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <p>The trick is always the same: look for the largest perfect square that divides evenly into your number, pull it out, and the rest stays under the radical sign.</p>
            </div>
        </section>

